{"text": "A year ago, I called myself a \"vibe coder, \" and technically I still vibe code, which, wait, terrible name for what this actually is. But that's where I started. I don't sit down and manually write every line of software I build. I work with AI, I give agents problems, I direct them, I inspect what they produce, I reject a lot of it, I change the architecture, and we build again. But somewhere along the way, the problems changed. I stopped asking \"How do I- I built this app and started asking: How should all of these systems work together? Who owns the memory? Which agent gets access? How does one agent hand work to another? How do I know an action actually happened? What stops an agent from exceeding its authority? And what does a human need to see to remain in control? That last one, that's the Jedi Council problem: Who gets a lightsaber? Who doesn't? Who thinks they should? Anyway, those weren't vibe coding problems anymore. They were systems problems, and solving them became agentopolis. My role changed too; I don't need to be the best Python programmer, Rust engineer, product designer, and 3D developer simultaneously. That's not how I work. I build teams of intelligence around the problem: one agent researches, another implements, another reviews, another challenges the assumptions. Hermes provides persistent agents and coordination. Coding agents work directly against repositories. And I stay above the system, deciding what belongs, what doesn't, and where the architecture needs to change. AI doesn't eliminate expertise, it changes where the human expertise has to be applied. This is another part of working with AI that doesn't get shown enough. An agent can satisfy the requirements and still completely miss the idea. This version technically worked, the information was there, the interface rendered, the deployment worked, and I hated it. It looked like an enterprise app. This was the part in the movie where everybody else thinks the machine is working fine, and I'm yelling at the screen because I can already see act three. Agentropolis wasn't supposed to feel like an enterprise dashboard, so the problem wasn't code, the problem was direction. I pulled parallax back up as the visual reference, rewrote the design canon, changed the acceptance criteria, and sent the system through another iteration. That's what directing AI actually looks like. Generation is cheap, judgement is cheap. Isn't. Parallax came from the same philosophy: never confuse generation with proof. An agent can tell me something is finished, I want the system to inspect the result, verify the objective, and leave evidence. C. Act. See again, prove it. That idea started with spatial environments, but it became one of my principles for agentic systems: generated doesn't equal verified. You don't hand somebody the Death Star and skip asking who has the keys. I also didn't build this inside a research laboratory. There wasn't an engineering department waiting downstairs. I'm a solo founder. That constraint forced a different question: how much organizational capability can one person assemble using agents? Research. Architecture, code, design, documentation, testing, operations, creative production, not by pretending I personally possess every specialization, but by learning how to coordinate machines that increasingly do. At that point it was basically \"push it, \" except I was pushing agents across the entire architecture. Agentropolis became the experiment. So what do I actually call what I do now? Probably agentic systems architecture with a lot of creative technology mixed in. I work between the idea and the implementation. I turn emerging capabilities into systems, I design agent roles and authority boundaries, I decide what gets delegated to machines and what stays under human control, then I use agents to help execute the architecture. I'm still learning, the technology is still changing, but that's also why I'm building this now. I'm not convinced the next generation of software will look like today's applications. I think we're moving towards systems populated by By persistent agents, different models, different machines, different owners, different levels of authority, working together across environments. So now I've got agents talking to agents across machines, and I'm sitting there thinking, \"Okay, this is starting to look less like an app and more like the rebel fleet. If that's where we're going, the hard problem isn't simply making the agents smarter, it's designing the systems they operate inside. That's the problem I'm interested in. That's what I've spent this year learning how to solve. How to build an agentropolis is my working answer, not a finished answer, a system that's alive enough to keep changing as the technology changes. I started this as a vibe coder, I ended up becoming an architect of agent systems, and I'm just getting started.", "language": "en", "duration": 308.16, "words": [{"text": "A", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.16}, {"text": "year", "start": 0.26, "end": 0.46}, {"text": "ago,", "start": 0.52, "end": 0.68}, {"text": "I", "start": 1.081, "end": 1.101}, {"text": "called", "start": 1.161, "end": 1.421}, {"text": "myself", "start": 1.461, "end": 1.841}, {"text": "a", "start": 1.901, "end": 1.921}, {"text": "\"vibe", "start": 2.041, "end": 2.382}, {"text": "coder,", "start": 2.422, "end": 2.762}, {"text": "\"", "start": 2.762, "end": 3.322}, {"text": "and", "start": 3.322, "end": 3.422}, {"text": "technically", "start": 3.482, "end": 4.003}, {"text": "I", "start": 4.243, "end": 4.263}, {"text": "still", "start": 4.363, "end": 4.603}, {"text": "vibe", "start": 4.683, "end": 5.003}, {"text": "code,", "start": 5.023, "end": 5.404}, {"text": "which,", "start": 5.784, "end": 6.044}, {"text": "wait,", "start": 6.304, "end": 6.564}, {"text": "terrible", "start": 6.985, "end": 7.425}, {"text": "name", "start": 7.465, "end": 7.725}, {"text": "for", "start": 7.765, "end": 7.865}, {"